1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,864
Aziz opens the mobile bank at breakfast to check his balance.

2
00:00:03,914 --> 00:00:06,696
He opens it again at lunch, and once more at night.

3
00:00:06,746 --> 00:00:08,448
He never types his password.

4
00:00:08,498 --> 00:00:11,976
So what lets him in, again and again, without a login?

5
00:00:12,026 --> 00:00:18,720
In Lesson 20, his phone got a record and a state, and the gateway checked the binding at each request.

6
00:00:18,770 --> 00:00:25,728
This lesson shows what the app holds when it calls: the access grant, its tokens, its level and its renewal.

7
00:00:25,778 --> 00:00:27,792
An access grant is a session.

8
00:00:27,842 --> 00:00:32,136
It belongs to one app account, on one device, for one customer.

9
00:00:32,186 --> 00:00:35,952
Aziz got his first grant on registration day, at level one.

10
00:00:36,002 --> 00:00:38,184
He gets a new one after each login.

11
00:00:38,234 --> 00:00:42,288
Two parts of the platform take part, and they have very different jobs.

12
00:00:42,338 --> 00:00:44,328
The customer gateway decides.

13
00:00:44,378 --> 00:00:48,504
It checks the password, the code, the verdict and the device binding.

14
00:00:48,554 --> 00:00:48,864
Keycloak only issues.

15
00:00:48,914 --> 00:00:52,968
When the gateway is satisfied, it signs a short note for Keycloak.

16
00:00:53,018 --> 00:00:58,608
The note says three things: this device, this customer, this session level.

17
00:00:58,658 --> 00:01:02,232
It is valid for a very short time, and only once.

18
00:01:02,282 --> 00:01:08,328
Keycloak reads the note and issues two tokens: an access token and a refresh token.

19
00:01:08,378 --> 00:01:10,200
I think this is the right split.

20
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:17,496
Keycloak is a well-known engine for tokens, but it knows nothing about TAF, MyID or the bank's rules.

21
00:01:17,546 --> 00:01:23,688
All those rules stay in the gateway, so a new version of Keycloak never changes how the bank decides.

22
00:01:23,738 --> 00:01:25,920
Now look at what the access token says.

23
00:01:25,970 --> 00:01:27,672
First, it names its own app.

24
00:01:27,722 --> 00:01:32,448
A mobile bank token never opens the marketplace, as Lesson 5 showed.

25
00:01:32,498 --> 00:01:36,288
Second, it carries the session level: one or two.

26
00:01:36,338 --> 00:01:41,568
The gateway writes level two only when the customer is at level two and the device is Trusted.

27
00:01:41,618 --> 00:01:45,912
On registration day, Aziz's token said level one.

28
00:01:45,962 --> 00:01:52,296
A week later he passed MyID on this phone, and the gateway issued a new token with level two at once.

29
00:01:52,346 --> 00:01:54,720
He did not wait for the old token to run out.

30
00:01:54,770 --> 00:02:00,024
So any app, and any backend of the bank, can read the level straight from the token.

31
00:02:00,074 --> 00:02:01,896
Back to breakfast.

32
00:02:01,946 --> 00:02:05,808
The access token lives only a short time, on purpose.

33
00:02:05,858 --> 00:02:09,336
When it runs out, the app does not ask Aziz for anything.

34
00:02:09,386 --> 00:02:13,728
It sends the refresh token to the gateway, with its device identifier.

35
00:02:13,778 --> 00:02:15,600
This is a renewal.

36
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:19,488
The gateway renews only for the same device and the same app.

37
00:02:19,538 --> 00:02:24,000
Before anything else, the renewal passes the binding check from Lesson 20.

38
00:02:24,050 --> 00:02:26,880
Then the gateway looks at the revocation list.

39
00:02:26,930 --> 00:02:31,824
This list holds every grant that was ended, and the gateway reads it at each call.

40
00:02:31,874 --> 00:02:38,208
If both checks pass, Keycloak gives a fresh access token, and a fresh refresh token too.

41
00:02:38,258 --> 00:02:40,800
The old refresh token is now used up.

42
00:02:40,850 --> 00:02:45,000
If someone tries to use it a second time, Keycloak sees the reuse.

43
00:02:45,050 --> 00:02:47,232
A renewal needs no password.

44
00:02:47,282 --> 00:02:51,072
The password opens a login, and a renewal is not a login.

45
00:02:51,122 --> 00:02:52,944
It only continues one.

46
00:02:52,994 --> 00:02:54,816
There is one more limit.

47
00:02:54,866 --> 00:03:00,696
When Aziz leaves the app alone for longer than the inactivity limit, the app asks for his app PIN.

48
00:03:00,746 --> 00:03:02,568
The grant does not end.

49
00:03:02,618 --> 00:03:05,640
The PIN only unlocks the app on this phone again.

50
00:03:05,690 --> 00:03:11,616
The bank sets the full lifetime of a grant, and its inactivity limit, as policies.

51
00:03:11,666 --> 00:03:14,376
Now the other side: how a grant ends.

52
00:03:14,426 --> 00:03:17,640
Last year Aziz used the mobile bank on a tablet.

53
00:03:17,690 --> 00:03:19,512
This week he cannot find it.

54
00:03:19,562 --> 00:03:23,160
He calls the bank, and an operator revokes the tablet.

55
00:03:23,210 --> 00:03:27,624
The grant of the tablet ends at its next request, not at its next renewal.

56
00:03:27,674 --> 00:03:30,240
If the tablet calls, it is refused.

57
00:03:30,290 --> 00:03:33,648
If it tries to renew, the revocation list says no.

58
00:03:33,698 --> 00:03:46,584
A grant can end in a few more ways: a block of the customer, a binding mismatch, a replaced phone, a phone number that was removed, a password reset or change on a different device, or simply its lifetime.

59
00:03:46,634 --> 00:03:50,736
In each case, the app gets the same answer: this grant has ended.

60
00:03:50,786 --> 00:03:52,272
The app tries a renewal.

61
00:03:52,322 --> 00:03:55,776
When the renewal fails too, Aziz must log in again.

62
00:03:55,826 --> 00:03:57,648
To recap.

63
00:03:57,698 --> 00:04:00,816
The gateway decides, and Keycloak issues.

64
00:04:00,866 --> 00:04:03,120
The token names its app and its level.

65
00:04:03,170 --> 00:04:06,432
A renewal keeps the session alive with no password.

66
00:04:06,482 --> 00:04:10,896
And one revocation stops a device at its very next request.

67
00:04:10,946 --> 00:04:12,768
But look at the marketplace.

68
00:04:12,818 --> 00:04:20,064
Aziz's marketplace token says level one, and the page he wants shows his orders, which needs level two.

69
00:04:20,114 --> 00:04:22,152
Should the marketplace refuse him?

70
00:04:22,202 --> 00:04:24,864
What does an app do with a level that is too low?

71
00:04:24,914 --> 00:04:26,352
Next: step-up.
